Conducting a Security Audit and Risk Assessment

Step 1: Check System Information
Steps:
Press Windows + R
Type:
msinfo32
Check:
OS Version
System Type
Secure Boot Status
Observation:
Windows 11 (64-bit)
Intel i5 processor
UEFI architecture
Secure Boot enabled
Analysis:
Secure Boot prevents unauthorized boot loaders and improves system integrity.

Step 2: Check Windows Updates
Steps:
Open Settings → Windows Update
Click Check for updates
Observation:
Updates available / pending
Analysis:
Pending updates expose the system to known vulnerabilities.
Regular updates are critical for security.

Step 3: Check Firewall Status
Steps:
Search: Windows Defender Firewall
Click Turn Windows Defender Firewall on/off
Observation:
Managed by Kaspersky (third-party firewall)
Analysis:
A third-party firewall enhances protection, but misconfiguration can create risks.

Step 4: Check Antivirus Status
Steps:
Open Windows Security
Go to Virus & Threat Protection
Check:
Real-time protection → ON
Last scan → Recent
Observation:
Kaspersky antivirus active
No threats detected
Analysis:
Active antivirus protects against malware, phishing, and threats.

Step 5: Check Account & Password Security
Steps:
Settings → Accounts → Sign-in options
Check:
Password / PIN enabled
Windows Hello (optional)
Observation:
Password + PIN authentication enabled
Analysis:
Multi-factor authentication improves access security.

Step 6: Check Installed Applications
Steps:
Settings → Apps → Installed apps
Look for:
Unknown software
Cracked tools
Observation:
No suspicious applications found
Analysis:
Unauthorized software may introduce malware.

Step 7: Check Startup Programs
Steps:
Press Ctrl + Shift + Esc
Go to Startup tab
Action:
Disable unknown applications
Observation:
Some apps enabled at startup
Analysis:
Reducing startup apps:
Improves performance
Reduces attack surface

Step 8: Check Network Security
Steps:
Settings → Network & Internet
Check:
Wi-Fi status
Network profile (Public/Private)
Observation:
Connected to Wi-Fi
Network type: Public
Analysis:
Public network:
Limits device visibility
Improves security in untrusted environments

Step 9: Check Browser Security
Steps (Chrome/Edge):
Settings → Privacy & Security
Check:
Safe Browsing → ON
Remove unknown extensions
Observation:
Safe browsing enabled
Analysis:
Protects against:
Phishing
Malicious downloads

Step 10: Check Data Backup
Steps:
Search: Backup settings
Check:
OneDrive / External backup
Observation:
Backup not configured
Analysis:
No backup = high risk of data loss (e.g., ransomware attacks)

5. Risk Assessment Table
Asset	Threat	Vulnerability	Risk Level	Solution
Personal Files	Data Loss	No Backup	High	Enable OneDrive
System	Malware	Unknown Apps	High	Remove apps
Network	Hacking	Public Network	Medium	Use Private
Account	Unauthorized Access	Weak Password	High	Strong password

6. Security Recommendations
✔ Technical Controls
Enable firewall
Enable antivirus
Perform regular updates
✔ Administrative Controls
Use strong passwords
Avoid installing unknown software
✔ User Awareness
Avoid phishing links
Practice safe browsing
